    "lesson": {
      "title": "IPv4 Networking Basics",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "IPv4 Address Structure",
          "text": "An IPv4 address consists of 32 bits. These bits are divided into a network portion and a host portion. The length of the network portion is indicated by a prefix, such as /26.",
          "source_refs": [
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:0",
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Calculating Host Bits and Addresses",
          "text": "The prefix determines the number of host bits. For example, a /26 prefix means that 26 bits are for the network, leaving 6 bits for hosts (32 - 26 = 6). A block with 6 host bits can accommodate 2^6 = 64 addresses.",
          "source_refs": [
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:1",
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:2",
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Network, Broadcast, and Usable Host Addresses",
          "text": "Within a network block, specific addresses are reserved. For an address like 192.168.1.70/26, the network address is 192.168.1.64 and the broadcast address is 192.168.1.127. The usable host addresses range from 192.168.1.65 to 192.168.1.126, providing 62 usable addresses.",
          "source_refs": [
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:3",
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:4",
            "00055136cfaa4d0ba7281e0ff0a9cadf:2:5"
          ]
        }
      ],
